[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: scribe has no --dry-run

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[WORK]
UID: WORK-SCRIBE-DRY-RUN
TITLE: scribe has no --dry-run
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
scribe grows --dry-run on every writing verb: new (every type leaf), set,
relate, unrelate, move, delete. The flag is added once, by walking the built
subparsers for every name in scribe_ops.WRITES, and folded into the RPC payload
once, not per verb. A dry run performs everything the real run does up to the
write: it applies the change in memory, runs the same prechecks, and proves
every touched document renders and reparses. Then it prints a unified diff of
what would be written, path by path, computed from Graph.pending() against the
file's current bytes, and writes nothing. An empty diff prints that nothing
would change. A change the real run would refuse is refused by the dry run with
the same message and exit code. For move, the dry run prints the rename it would
perform and any content it would rewrite. After a dry run the daemon's next read
is identical to before it; how that is achieved (discard and reload, or mutate a
copy) is chosen by measurement and recorded on this node. The diff helper is
shared between scribe and sdoc_cli.py rather than copied. The sdoc skill and
docs/sdoc/README.md, which today document the absence, say the flag exists and
what it prints.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Implemented 2026-09-03. The client walks the built parser leaves named by
scribe_ops.WRITES, adds --dry-run in one place, and folds dry_run into every
write payload in one place. Both writer CLIs use scribe_diff.py for byte-based,
path-by-path unified diffs. Workspace.write runs the ordinary mutation,
precheck, render and reparse pipeline, captures Graph.pending(), then discards
the speculative generation instead of saving it.

The held-state choice was measured on this 495-document corpus. Across three
runs, capturing and discarding a no-change preview averaged 5.0 ms and the next
full read averaged 1,134.2 ms (800.6, 1,301.8 and 1,300.2 ms). A deepcopy of the
held graph took 4,444.9 ms in one run, about 3.9 times the discard/reload cost,
so copying was rejected. The focused suites pass 30 contracts: 20 workspace and
operation contracts plus 10 client and RPC contracts.
<<<
